<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Western Wei</h1><p class="st-meta">535 CE – 557 CE · capital Chang'an</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The western half of the divided Northern Wei, controlled by Yuwen Tai, whose militia system and Guanlong elite laid the foundations of the Sui and Tang.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuwen-tai">Yuwen Tai</a>, a general of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/xianbei">Xianbei</a> descent, controlled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guanzhong">Guanzhong</a> region around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a>. When the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/northern-wei">Northern Wei</a> emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-xiaowu-of-northern-wei">Xiaowu</a> fled to him in 534, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/yuwen-xianbei">Yuwen</a> Tai received him, then had him poisoned and in 535 installed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wen-of-western-wei">Yuan Baoju</a> as Emperor Wen of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/western-wei-206-bc">Western Wei</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/western-wei-206-bc">Western Wei</a> was poorer and smaller than <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/eastern-wei">Eastern Wei</a>, so <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuwen-tai">Yuwen Tai</a> built a new system. With the minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/su-chuo">Su Chuo</a> he issued administrative reforms, revived offices modelled on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rites-of-zhou">Rites of Zhou</a> and created the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fubing-system">fubing</a> militia, enrolling Chinese and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/xianbei">Xianbei</a> soldiers under Eight Pillars of State. This forged the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guanlong-aristocracy">Guanlong aristocracy</a>. Western Wei took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sichuan">Sichuan</a> in 553 and sacked the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/liang-dynasty">Liang dynasty</a>'s capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiangling">Jiangling</a> in 554.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuwen-tai">Yuwen Tai</a> died in 556, and in 557 his nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuwen-hu">Yuwen Hu</a> had the last emperor abdicate to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/yuwen-xianbei">Yuwen</a> Tai's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuwen-jue">Yuwen Jue</a>, founding the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/northern-zhou">Northern Zhou</a>. The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sui-dynasty">Sui Dynasty</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/tang-dynasty">Tang Dynasty</a> founders came from Western Wei's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guanlong-aristocracy">Guanlong</a> elite.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 535 CE – 557 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:70.35%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>535 CE – 557 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Chang'an</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Emperor Wen of Western Wei, Yuwen Tai, Emperor Gong of Western Wei</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Middle Chinese, Xianbei</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>China</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Wei (Yuwen Tai)</dd></dl></aside></div>
